{
 "id": "chinese-room",
 "channel": "philosophy",
 "style": "ps2",
 "title": "THE CHINESE ROOM",
 "angle": "If a machine talks exactly like us, does it understand a single word?",
 "logline": "A chatbot now passes for human better than humans do, so we ask if it understands, but Searle showed a man can answer in Chinese without knowing a word, so programs only shuffle symbols, but critics said the whole room understands, so Searle memorised the room and still understood nothing, but Turing had warned we can't see understanding in anyone.",
 "hook_text": "MORE HUMAN THAN HUMANS",
 "hooks": [
  "A man answers in perfect Chinese without knowing a word of it.",
  "Cognitive science was once jokingly defined as proving one man wrong."
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "default"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "In 2025, a chatbot was judged human more often than real humans.",
   "beat": "hook",
   "say": "In twenty twenty-five, a chatbot was judged human more often than real humans."
  },
  {
   "text": "Judges picked it as the human 73 percent of the time.",
   "beat": "hook",
   "link": "detail",
   "say": "Judges picked it as the human seventy-three percent of the time."
  },
  {
   "text": "So does a machine that talks like us understand a single word?",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "One philosopher spent 45 years saying no.",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "answer",
   "say": "One philosopher spent forty-five years saying no."
  },
  {
   "text": "In 1980, John Searle imagined himself locked in a room.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "because",
   "say": "In nineteen eighty, John Surl imagined himself locked in a room."
  },
  {
   "text": "Notes in Chinese slide under the door, and he speaks no Chinese.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "A rule book in English tells him which symbols to pass back out.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So his answers look fluent, yet he understands nothing.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But Searle wasn't talking about rooms: he meant computers.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but",
   "say": "But Surl wasn't talking about rooms: he meant computers."
  },
  {
   "text": "A program follows rules about symbols without knowing what they mean.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So by his logic, Deep Blue beat the world chess champion without knowing it played chess.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But critics answered: you don't understand Chinese, but the whole room does.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "One even defined cognitive science as the project of proving Searle wrong.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail",
   "say": "One even defined cognitive science as the project of proving Surl wrong."
  },
  {
   "text": "So Searle replied: memorize the whole rule book, and you still won't understand a word.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "so",
   "say": "So Surl replied: memorize the whole rule book, and you still won't understand a word."
  },
  {
   "text": "But Turing had already seen this coming in 1950.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "but",
   "say": "But Turing had already seen this coming in nineteen fifty."
  },
  {
   "text": "You can't see inside anyone's head, he wrote, so we politely assume everyone thinks.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So Searle's answer stayed no: he died in 2025, the year chatbots passed Turing's test.",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "answer",
   "say": "So Surl's answer stayed no: he died in twenty twenty-five, the year chatbots passed Turing's test."
  },
  {
   "text": "But if understanding can't be seen, how do you know anyone understands you?",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "but"
  }
 ],
